      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2013 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To evaluate the effects of interactions between ApoE genotypes, alcohol consumption and obesity on the age-related trends of blood pressure (BP) levels in postmenopausal women."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "A population-based prospective cohort study of all residents in Bambuì, south-eastern Brazil, aged 60 years or older. Repeated BP measurements were obtained in four waves from 851 women who underwent ApoE genotyping at baseline (88.3% of those enrolled), and multi-level random-effects pattern-mixture models were used to evaluate the age-related BP trajectories, while accounting for non-ignorable dropouts/deaths and handling heterogeneities as random parameter variations. The few measurements (2.1%) made during hormone replacement therapy were excluded from the analysis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Alcohol consumption was associated with high levels of systolic and diastolic BP in an age×genotype-dependent manner only in the non-obese women (BMI<27 kg/m(2)). Among those with the ɛ3/3 genotype, the differences in systolic and diastolic levels between drinkers and non-drinkers estimated at the age of 60 years were respectively 13.7 mmHg (p=0.022) and 10.7 mmHg (p=0.002), and disappeared in the older age groups, in which drinking was associated with systolic/diastolic hypertension if the non-obese women were ɛ4 carriers."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "In non-obese postmenopausal women, alcohol consumption is associated with systolic and diastolic hypertension early in those with the ɛ3/3 ApoE genotype, and late in ɛ4 carriers. We hypothesize the mediation of androgen hormones and the influence of ApoE genotypes on age at natural menopause. A better understanding of these mechanisms may guide better preventive choices."
          }
        ]
      },
